{
  "image_name": "0e513564403d7b8a832a99cf5db6b42f.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure gives a zoomed overview of how the kidney filters blood, moving from the whole organ down to the microscopic filtration barrier in a nephron. It starts with kidney anatomy, showing cortex, medulla, pelvis, ureter, and the branching renal blood vessels, then focuses on a nephron running from the cortex into the medulla. The nephron diagram highlights the afferent and efferent arterioles, glomerulus, proximal and distal tubules, loop of Henle, collecting duct, and surrounding peritubular capillaries, emphasizing the relationship between blood flow and tubular flow.\n\nThe central message is that filtration begins in the renal corpuscle, where blood enters the glomerulus and fluid passes into Bowman’s space, while later segments of the nephron handle reabsorption and secretion. The enlarged glomerulus shows key cell types and structures involved in this process, including podocytes, endothelial cells, mesangial cells, and the glomerular basement membrane. The final close-up of the glomerular filtration membrane illustrates the layered barrier between blood and Bowman’s space: fenestrated endothelium with glycocalyx, the basement membrane, and podocyte foot processes separated by narrow slit pores. Overall, the figure links kidney structure to the mechanism of selective filtration.",
